drumee-infra (1.2.30) stable; urgency=medium

  * Stop publishing a link-local IPv6 address in the zone. A LAN box whose only
    IPv6 address is the interface's link-local one published it as the AAAA of the
    apex, ns1, ns2, smtp, jit and the wildcard: the ip module classes fe80::/10 as
    private, so getAddresses selected it as private_ip6 and private.tpl emitted it
    seven times.
    .
    A link-local address is only meaningful together with an interface scope and an
    AAAA record has nowhere to put one, so the zone handed every client an address
    it cannot connect to. Measured on a real install: `curl -6 https://<domain>/`
    failed outright, and plain curl only survived because happy-eyeballs falls back
    to IPv4 after wasting a connection attempt on every request. An IPv6-only
    client, or any library without that fallback, cannot reach the instance at all.
    No AAAA is strictly better than an AAAA nothing can dial — clients then use the
    A record and get there.
    .
    Only visible now because 1.2.29 made the box resolve its own zone; before that
    nothing on the instance ever asked. The address was in the zone the whole time.
    .
    isPublishableIp6() rejects fe80::/10, ::1 and ::, and applies to
    operator-supplied values too: an explicit --private-ip6 fe80::1 is no more
    publishable than a detected one. fd00::/8 stays publishable — unique-local is
    private in the same sense as 192.168/16, and usable by every client that can
    reach the box at all.
    .
    Guarded by tests/native/dns-zone-config.sh, which now exercises the shipped
    predicate itself rather than a copy, asserts no rendered zone contains a
    link-local AAAA, and gained an ip6 case so the templates' AAAA branch is
    rendered and handed to a real named-checkconf for the first time — every other
    case leaves both ip6 values empty.

 -- somanos.sar@drumee.com  Fri, 07 Aug 2026 18:24:34 +0200

drumee-infra (1.2.29) stable; urgency=medium

  * Make 1.2.28's resolver fix survive a reboot. It did not, and the failure was
    silent in exactly the way that matters: the install measured as working.
    .
    ipv4.dns is an ORDERED list and NetworkManager writes resolv.conf in that
    order. `nmcli +ipv4.dns 127.0.0.1` appends, so the profile became
    "8.8.8.8,127.0.0.1" while the live resolv.conf edit still put 127.0.0.1 first
    — on-box resolution worked, and was verified working. On the next boot NM
    regenerated resolv.conf from the profile, in profile order, and the box asked
    8.8.8.8 for its own domain. glibc does not fall through to the next server on
    NXDOMAIN, only on refusal or timeout, so an authoritative "no such name" from
    a public resolver is final: on-box resolution was broken again with nothing in
    any log to say so.
    .
    The whole list is now rewritten with 127.0.0.1 first and the previous servers
    kept behind it. Loopback connections are skipped — 1.2.28 wrote to `lo` too,
    which is harmless but not ours to modify.
  * Report whether the host can resolve its own domain, asked through the SYSTEM
    resolver (getent hosts) rather than by querying named directly. That
    distinction is the lesson from the above: a check that talked to 127.0.0.1
    explicitly would have passed while nsswitch was broken. On failure it says so
    and prints the resolver state, instead of leaving the operator to discover it
    the next time an on-box probe fails.

 -- somanos.sar@drumee.com  Fri, 07 Aug 2026 18:12:46 +0200

drumee-infra (1.2.28) stable; urgency=medium

  * Make the host resolve the zone it serves. A LAN instance came up with named
    answering the domain for the whole network while /etc/resolv.conf still
    pointed at the DHCP resolver, so the box could not resolve its own name:
    on-box `curl https://<domain>/` returned nothing while the identical request
    from any other machine on the LAN got the site. Everything on-box that refers
    to the instance by name was resolving against a resolver that has never heard
    of the domain — drumee-ctl doctor's TLS probe, health checks, the
    password-reset URL in the welcome email if it is followed locally.
    .
    bin/init-named now points the host at 127.0.0.1 after named starts, but only
    after asking that named for the zone's SOA — named-checkconf passes
    configurations whose zone files do not load, and pointing the resolver at a
    named that came up empty would be worse than leaving it alone. It is added as
    the FIRST nameserver with the existing ones kept behind it, because glibc
    moves to the next server on refusal or timeout: a named that is down then
    costs a retry rather than all DNS. Safe because the rendered
    named.conf.options sets `recursion yes` with public forwarders, so 127.0.0.1
    is a complete resolver and not an authoritative island.
    .
    Persisted per resolver manager, since editing the file alone is a fix that
    lasts until the next reboot: a systemd-resolved drop-in routing only this
    domain to the local named (split DNS, narrowest possible change), or
    `nmcli +ipv4.dns` which prepends to what DHCP supplies rather than replacing
    it. Deliberately no `nmcli con up` afterwards — on the documented install path
    this runs over ssh, and bouncing the connection carrying the install to apply
    a DNS entry is a poor trade; the live file is edited directly for the
    immediate half.
  * Recommend bind9-dnsutils. postinst ends the DNS path by printing "Check it
    with: dig +short <domain> @<address>" and on a fresh install that command did
    not exist — neither bind9 nor bind9-utils pulls dig in.

