drumee-infra (1.2.27) stable; urgency=medium

  * Make `dpkg-reconfigure drumee-infra` actually re-render. It was a silent
    no-op, on exactly the hosts it exists for.
    .
    setup-infra guards against clobbering an installed host:
    hasExistingSettings() reads /etc/drumee/drumee.json and, if a domain_name is
    already there, refuses — and infra.js then calls exit(0), so the caller sees
    success. Re-rendering is allowed only when it was explicitly agreed to, which
    the guard tests as `args.reconfigure` (its own message says "Use
    --reconfigure=1", and the argparse default reads DRUMEE_RECONFIGURE=1 "so a
    package can drive it").
    .
    This postinst passed `--force-install=1` instead — an option that is declared
    with the help text "Override existing configs" and read by nothing. So the
    guard held, infra.js exited 0, dpkg reported success, and not one file was
    re-rendered. Measured against the setup-infra bundled in 1.2.26:
    --force-install=1 blocks, --reconfigure=1 proceeds.
    .
    Consequence while it lasted: no configuration fix could reach an installed
    host, and every TLS/DNS message in this script ends by advising
    "then: dpkg-reconfigure drumee-infra" — advice that did nothing. Note this is
    the mechanism, not the policy: re-rendering still overwrites hand edits under
    /etc/drumee, the nginx vhosts, the BIND zones, Prosody and Postfix, which is
    what the debconf reconfigure_existing question warns about.
    .
    Also fixed upstream in setup-infra, which now accepts --force-install as an
    alias for --reconfigure, so an already-deployed postinst passing the old flag
    starts working too.

