drumee-infra (1.2.25) stable; urgency=medium

  * Reload nginx after rendering its vhosts. Nothing did — neither postinst nor
    setup-infra/bin/install (grep either for nginx: they write the files and never
    tell nginx about them). nginx's own postinst has already started it by the time
    we are configured, with the configuration that existed *before* the render, so
    the process stays up carrying no Drumee vhost and the instance serves nothing:
    `systemctl is-active nginx` reports active while curl gets connection-refused,
    because there is no listener on 80 or 443 at all.
    .
    A fresh `apt install drumee` therefore completed, reported success, started the
    backend on 23000/24000 — and answered on no public port until somebody ran
    `systemctl restart nginx` by hand. Found by wiping a box and installing from
    scratch; every deployment that looked healthy had been restarted at some point,
    which is exactly what kept this hidden.
    .
    The reload is refused rather than attempted when `nginx -t` fails, and the test
    output is printed: a failed reload leaves the old workers serving, which here
    means still serving nothing, with the reason buried in postinst output. A
    stopped unit is started rather than reloaded, since reloading a dead unit is a
    silent no-op.

