drumee-infra (1.2.24) stable; urgency=medium

  * Clean the rendered nginx configuration on `remove`, not only on `purge`.
    `remove` deletes the files this package ships under /etc/drumee while leaving
    the vhosts that unconditionally *include* them, so nginx then refuses its
    whole configuration:
    .
      nginx: [emerg] open() "/etc/drumee/ssl/public.conf" failed (2: No such
             file or directory) in /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/01-public.conf:36
    .
    Which no reinstall can recover from, because every Drumee package Depends on
    nginx: nginx cannot configure until drumee-infra re-renders /etc/drumee/ssl,
    and drumee-infra cannot configure until nginx does. `apt remove drumee`
    followed by `apt install drumee` deadlocked precisely there and left the
    entire stack unpacked-but-unconfigured. Observed on a real box, not inferred.
    Nothing is cleaned on `upgrade`: the old postrm runs before the new postinst
    re-renders, so deleting the vhosts there would strip nginx of them mid-upgrade.
  * Re-enable nginx's stream module before rendering a stream block. Debian's
    libnginx-mod-* prerm parks its load_module link as `<name>.conf.removed` when
    the module package is removed, and our 90-turn-relay.*.conf lands a `stream {}`
    block in modules-enabled — which nginx includes at MAIN context, so a parked
    link costs the instance all of nginx:
    .
      nginx: [emerg] unknown directive "stream" in
             /etc/nginx/modules-enabled/90-turn-relay.public.conf:15
    .
    Depending on libnginx-mod-stream does not fix this: dpkg configures nginx
    first (nginx provides the nginx-abi-* the module needs), so `nginx -t` runs
    inside nginx's postinst while the module is still parked. postinst now
    restores the link before anything renders a stream block.

 -- Somanos Sar <somanos.sar@drumee.com>  Mon, 03 Aug 2026 13:01:09 +0200

drumee-infra (1.2.23) stable; urgency=medium

  * Make tls_method=self-signed produce a serving instance. It could not: nginx
    failed to load and nothing was served. Found by wiping a test box and
    reinstalling from scratch — the deployments before it had all inherited a
    hand-built /etc/drumee/ssl tree from an earlier tls_method=own install, which
    had been masking this the whole time.
    .
    create-local-certs signed only the private and conferencing names, while
    infra.js renders the public and vendors vhosts whenever a domain is set,
    whatever the TLS method — so the apex and vendors names had no certificate.
    Those vhosts also read the acme.sh layout (fullchain.cer + ca.cer), which
    until now only ACME, Caddy's exporter or an operator's own certs produced.
    Every name now gets both layouts, since the templates disagree about which
    they read, and names with no rendered .cnf are no longer skipped.
  * Skip ACME on the self-signed path. A private domain cannot answer a DNS-01
    challenge, so init-acme was eleven retries with sleeps between — minutes of
    guaranteed failure on every install — and it registered an ACME account for
    an address that will never validate. postinst exports DRUMEE_TLS_METHOD for
    this; unset keeps the previous behaviour for out-of-tree callers.
  * The self-signed certificates were also ones no client would accept. The
    rendered openssl configs carry `CN = *.<domain>` and no subjectAltName at
    all; every current browser ignores CN and requires a SAN, and a wildcard CN
    would not have covered the apex regardless. The config is now generated with
    `DNS:<domain>, DNS:*.<domain>`.
  * Two quieter fixes in the same script: the trust-store copy used a .cer
    extension while update-ca-certificates only considers *.crt, so none of these
    certificates was ever trusted by the host (5 added now, where it reported 0);
    and the private key was being copied world-readable into
    /usr/local/share/ca-certificates, a directory that exists to hold public
    trust material.

